#, no-wrap
msgid "C library/kernel differences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:282
msgid ""
"There are two underlying Linux system calls: B<eventfd>()  and the more "
"recent B<eventfd2>().  The former system call does not implement a I<flags> "
"argument.  The latter system call implements the I<flags> values described "
"above.  The glibc wrapper function will use B<eventfd2>()  where it is "
"available."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:282
#, no-wrap
msgid "Additional glibc features"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:286
msgid ""
"The GNU C library defines an additional type, and two functions that attempt "
"to abstract some of the details of reading and writing on an eventfd file "
"descriptor:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:293
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"typedef uint64_t eventfd_t;\n"
"\\&\n"
"int eventfd_read(int fd, eventfd_t *value);\n"
"int eventfd_write(int fd, eventfd_t value);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:300
msgid ""
"The functions perform the read and write operations on an eventfd file "
"descriptor, returning 0 if the correct number of bytes was transferred, or "
"-1 otherwise."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:302
msgid "Linux, GNU."
msgstr ""

#.  signalfd() is in glibc 2.7, but reportedly does not build
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:308 build/C/man2/signalfd.2:371
msgid "Linux 2.6.22, glibc 2.8."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:308
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<eventfd2>()"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:316
msgid ""
"Linux 2.6.27 (see VERSIONS).  Since glibc 2.9, the B<eventfd>()  wrapper "
"will employ the B<eventfd2>()  system call, if it is supported by the "
"kernel."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:316 build/C/man2/getitimer.2:199
#: build/C/man2/kill.2:139 build/C/man2/restart_syscall.2:84
#: build/C/man2/rt_sigqueueinfo.2:179 build/C/man2/s390_runtime_instr.2:87
#: build/C/man2/sgetmask.2:59 build/C/man2/sigaction.2:1095
#: build/C/man2/sigaltstack.2:249 build/C/man2/signal.2:226
#: build/C/man7/signal.7:921 build/C/man2/signalfd.2:374
#: build/C/man2/sigpending.2:75 build/C/man2/sigprocmask.2:176
#: build/C/man3/sigqueue.3:145 build/C/man2/sigreturn.2:59
#: build/C/man3/sigset.3:189 build/C/man3/sigsetops.3:174
#: build/C/man2/sigsuspend.2:98 build/C/man3/sigvec.3:253
#: build/C/man2/sigwaitinfo.2:164 build/C/man2/timer_create.2:271
#: build/C/man2/timerfd_create.2:482 build/C/man2/tkill.2:119
#: build/C/man2/wait.2:479
#, no-wrap
msgid "NOTES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:324
msgid ""
"Applications can use an eventfd file descriptor instead of a pipe (see "
"B<pipe>(2))  in all cases where a pipe is used simply to signal events.  The "
"kernel overhead of an eventfd file descriptor is much lower than that of a "
"pipe, and only one file descriptor is required (versus the two required for "
"a pipe)."
msgstr ""

#.  or eventually syslets/threadlets
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:330
msgid ""
"When used in the kernel, an eventfd file descriptor can provide a bridge "
"from kernel to user space, allowing, for example, functionalities like KAIO "
"(kernel AIO)  to signal to a file descriptor that some operation is "
"complete."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:347
msgid ""
"A key point about an eventfd file descriptor is that it can be monitored "
"just like any other file descriptor using B<select>(2), B<poll>(2), or "
"B<epoll>(7).  This means that an application can simultaneously monitor the "
"readiness of \"traditional\" files and the readiness of other kernel "
"mechanisms that support the eventfd interface.  (Without the B<eventfd>()  "
"interface, these mechanisms could not be multiplexed via B<select>(2), "
"B<poll>(2), or B<epoll>(7).)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:355
msgid ""
"The current value of an eventfd counter can be viewed via the entry for the "
"corresponding file descriptor in the process's I</proc/>pidI</fdinfo> "
"directory.  See B<proc>(5)  for further details."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:355 build/C/man2/sigaction.2:1201
#: build/C/man2/sigaltstack.2:356 build/C/man2/signalfd.2:442
#: build/C/man3/sigwait.3:101 build/C/man2/timer_create.2:323
#: build/C/man2/timer_getoverrun.2:121 build/C/man2/timer_settime.2:180
#: build/C/man2/timerfd_create.2:535 build/C/man2/wait.2:612
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXAMPLES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:363
msgid ""
"The following program creates an eventfd file descriptor and then forks to "
"create a child process.  While the parent briefly sleeps, the child writes "
"each of the integers supplied in the program's command-line arguments to the "
"eventfd file descriptor.  When the parent has finished sleeping, it reads "
"from the eventfd file descriptor."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:365
msgid "The following shell session shows a sample run of the program:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:377
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$B< ./a.out 1 2 4 7 14>\n"
"Child writing 1 to efd\n"
"Child writing 2 to efd\n"
"Child writing 4 to efd\n"
"Child writing 7 to efd\n"
"Child writing 14 to efd\n"
"Child completed write loop\n"
"Parent about to read\n"
"Parent read 28 (0x1c) from efd\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:379 build/C/man2/signalfd.2:465
#: build/C/man2/timer_create.2:354 build/C/man2/timerfd_create.2:563
#: build/C/man2/wait.2:647
#, no-wrap
msgid "Program source"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:435
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include E<lt>err.hE<gt>\n"
"#include E<lt>inttypes.hE<gt>\n"
"#include E<lt>stdio.hE<gt>\n"
"#include E<lt>stdlib.hE<gt>\n"
"#include E<lt>sys/eventfd.hE<gt>\n"
"#include E<lt>sys/types.hE<gt>\n"
"#include E<lt>unistd.hE<gt>\n"
"\\&\n"
"int\n"
"main(int argc, char *argv[])\n"
"{\n"
"    int       efd;\n"
"    uint64_t  u;\n"
"    ssize_t   s;\n"
"\\&\n"
"    if (argc E<lt> 2) {\n"
"        fprintf(stderr, \"Usage: %s E<lt>numE<gt>...\\[rs]n\", argv[0]);\n"
"        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n"
"    }\n"
"\\&\n"
"    efd = eventfd(0, 0);\n"
"    if (efd == -1)\n"
"        err(EXIT_FAILURE, \"eventfd\");\n"
"\\&\n"
"    switch (fork()) {\n"
"    case 0:\n"
"        for (size_t j = 1; j E<lt> argc; j++) {\n"
"            printf(\"Child writing %s to efd\\[rs]n\", argv[j]);\n"
"            u = strtoull(argv[j], NULL, 0);\n"
"                    /* strtoull() allows various bases */\n"
"            s = write(efd, &u, sizeof(uint64_t));\n"
"            if (s != sizeof(uint64_t))\n"
"                err(EXIT_FAILURE, \"write\");\n"
"        }\n"
"        printf(\"Child completed write loop\\[rs]n\");\n"
"\\&\n"
"        exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n"
"\\&\n"
"    default:\n"
"        sleep(2);\n"
"\\&\n"
"        printf(\"Parent about to read\\[rs]n\");\n"
"        s = read(efd, &u, sizeof(uint64_t));\n"
"        if (s != sizeof(uint64_t))\n"
"            err(EXIT_FAILURE, \"read\");\n"
"        printf(\"Parent read %w64u (%#w64x) from efd\\[rs]n\", u, u);\n"
"        exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n"
"\\&\n"
"    case -1:\n"
"        err(EXIT_FAILURE, \"fork\");\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/eventfd.2:447
msgid ""
"B<futex>(2), B<pipe>(2), B<poll>(2), B<read>(2), B<select>(2), "
"B<signalfd>(2), B<timerfd_create>(2), B<write>(2), B<epoll>(7), "
"B<sem_overview>(7)"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:9
#, no-wrap
msgid "getitimer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:12
msgid "getitimer, setitimer - get or set value of an interval timer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:18
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<#include E<lt>sys/time.hE<gt>>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:22
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<int getitimer(int >I<which>B<, struct itimerval *>I<curr_value>B<);>\n"
"B<int setitimer(int >I<which>B<, const struct itimerval *restrict "
">I<new_value>B<,>\n"
"B<              struct itimerval *_Nullable restrict >I<old_value>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:30
msgid ""
"These system calls provide access to interval timers, that is, timers that "
"initially expire at some point in the future, and (optionally) at regular "
"intervals after that.  When a timer expires, a signal is generated for the "
"calling process, and the timer is reset to the specified interval (if the "
"interval is nonzero)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:36
msgid ""
"Three types of timers\\[em]specified via the I<which> argument\\[em]are "
"provided, each of which counts against a different clock and generates a "
"different signal on timer expiration:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:36
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<ITIMER_REAL>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:42
msgid ""
"This timer counts down in real (i.e., wall clock) time.  At each expiration, "
"a B<SIGALRM> signal is generated."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:42
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<ITIMER_VIRTUAL>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:49
msgid ""
"This timer counts down against the user-mode CPU time consumed by the "
"process.  (The measurement includes CPU time consumed by all threads in the "
"process.)  At each expiration, a B<SIGVTALRM> signal is generated."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:49
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<ITIMER_PROF>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:57
msgid ""
"This timer counts down against the total (i.e., both user and system)  CPU "
"time consumed by the process.  (The measurement includes CPU time consumed "
"by all threads in the process.)  At each expiration, a B<SIGPROF> signal is "
"generated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:62
msgid ""
"In conjunction with B<ITIMER_VIRTUAL>, this timer can be used to profile "
"user and system CPU time consumed by the process."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:64
msgid "A process has only one of each of the three types of timers."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:66
msgid "Timer values are defined by the following structures:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:78
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct itimerval {\n"
"    struct timeval it_interval; /* Interval for periodic timer */\n"
"    struct timeval it_value;    /* Time until next expiration */\n"
"};\n"
"\\&\n"
"struct timeval {\n"
"    time_t      tv_sec;         /* seconds */\n"
"    suseconds_t tv_usec;        /* microseconds */\n"
"};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:81
#, no-wrap
msgid "getitimer()"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:88
msgid ""
"The function B<getitimer>()  places the current value of the timer specified "
"by I<which> in the buffer pointed to by I<curr_value>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:99
msgid ""
"The I<it_value> substructure is populated with the amount of time remaining "
"until the next expiration of the specified timer.  This value changes as the "
"timer counts down, and will be reset to I<it_interval> when the timer "
"expires.  If both fields of I<it_value> are zero, then this timer is "
"currently disarmed (inactive)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:106
msgid ""
"The I<it_interval> substructure is populated with the timer interval.  If "
"both fields of I<it_interval> are zero, then this is a single-shot timer "
"(i.e., it expires just once)."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:106
#, no-wrap
msgid "setitimer()"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:119
msgid ""
"The function B<setitimer>()  arms or disarms the timer specified by "
"I<which>, by setting the timer to the value specified by I<new_value>.  If "
"I<old_value> is non-NULL, the buffer it points to is used to return the "
"previous value of the timer (i.e., the same information that is returned by "
"B<getitimer>())."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:127
msgid ""
"If either field in I<new_value.it_value> is nonzero, then the timer is armed "
"to initially expire at the specified time.  If both fields in "
"I<new_value.it_value> are zero, then the timer is disarmed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:132
msgid ""
"The I<new_value.it_interval> field specifies the new interval for the timer; "
"if both of its subfields are zero, the timer is single-shot."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:137 build/C/man2/tkill.2:85
#: build/C/man3/killpg.3:57
msgid ""
"On success, zero is returned.  On error, -1 is returned, and I<errno> is set "
"to indicate the error."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:138 build/C/man2/sigaction.2:973
#: build/C/man2/sigaltstack.2:202 build/C/man2/sigpending.2:41
#: build/C/man2/sigprocmask.2:104 build/C/man2/sigsuspend.2:59
#: build/C/man2/timer_settime.2:153 build/C/man2/timerfd_create.2:448
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<EFAULT>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:145 build/C/man2/timer_settime.2:160
#: build/C/man2/timerfd_create.2:455
msgid "I<new_value>, I<old_value>, or I<curr_value> is not a valid pointer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:158
msgid ""
"I<which> is not one of B<ITIMER_REAL>, B<ITIMER_VIRTUAL>, or B<ITIMER_PROF>; "
"or (since Linux 2.6.22) one of the I<tv_usec> fields in the structure "
"pointed to by I<new_value> contains a value outside the range [0, 999999]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:160
msgid "The standards are silent on the meaning of the call:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:164
#, no-wrap
msgid "setitimer(which, NULL, &old_value);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:169
msgid ""
"Many systems (Solaris, the BSDs, and perhaps others)  treat this as "
"equivalent to:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:173
#, no-wrap
msgid "getitimer(which, &old_value);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:183
msgid ""
"In Linux, this is treated as being equivalent to a call in which the "
"I<new_value> fields are zero; that is, the timer is disabled.  I<Don't use "
"this Linux misfeature>: it is nonportable and unnecessary."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:199
msgid ""
"4.2BSD, SVr4, SUSv1, POSIX.1-2001.  POSIX.1-2008 marked B<getitimer>()  and "
"B<setitimer>()  obsolete, recommending the use of the POSIX timers API "
"(B<timer_gettime>(2), B<timer_settime>(2), etc.) instead.  POSIX.1-2024 "
"removed them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:209
msgid ""
"Timers will never expire before the requested time, but may expire some "
"(short) time afterward, which depends on the system timer resolution and on "
"the system load; see B<time>(7).  (But see BUGS below.)  If the timer "
"expires while the process is active (always true for B<ITIMER_VIRTUAL>), the "
"signal will be delivered immediately when generated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:215
msgid ""
"A child created via B<fork>(2)  does not inherit its parent's interval "
"timers.  Interval timers are preserved across an B<execve>(2)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:225
msgid ""
"POSIX.1 leaves the interaction between B<setitimer>()  and the three "
"interfaces B<alarm>(2), B<sleep>(3), and B<usleep>(3)  unspecified."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:225 build/C/man2/kill.2:168
#: build/C/man3/profil.3:78 build/C/man3/psignal.3:105
#: build/C/man2/sigaction.2:1167 build/C/man2/sigaltstack.2:318
#: build/C/man7/signal.7:947 build/C/man2/signalfd.2:434
#: build/C/man2/sigpending.2:95 build/C/man3/sigset.3:223
#: build/C/man2/timer_getoverrun.2:99 build/C/man2/timerfd_create.2:529
#: build/C/man2/wait.2:597
#, no-wrap
msgid "BUGS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:234
msgid ""
"The generation and delivery of a signal are distinct, and only one instance "
"of each of the signals listed above may be pending for a process.  Under "
"very heavy loading, an B<ITIMER_REAL> timer may expire before the signal "
"from a previous expiration has been delivered.  The second signal in such an "
"event will be lost."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:249
msgid ""
"Before Linux 2.6.16, timer values are represented in jiffies.  If a request "
"is made set a timer with a value whose jiffies representation exceeds "
"B<MAX_SEC_IN_JIFFIES> (defined in I<include/linux/jiffies.h>), then the "
"timer is silently truncated to this ceiling value.  On Linux/i386 (where, "
"since Linux 2.6.13, the default jiffy is 0.004 seconds), this means that the "
"ceiling value for a timer is approximately 99.42 days.  Since Linux 2.6.16, "
"the kernel uses a different internal representation for times, and this "
"ceiling is removed."
msgstr ""

#.  4 Jul 2005: It looks like this bug may remain in Linux 2.4.x.
#. 	http://lkml.org/lkml/2005/7/1/165
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:256
msgid ""
"On certain systems (including i386), Linux kernels before Linux 2.6.12 have "
"a bug which will produce premature timer expirations of up to one jiffy "
"under some circumstances.  This bug is fixed in Linux 2.6.12."
msgstr ""

#.  Bugzilla report 25 Apr 2006:
#.  http://bugzilla.kernel.org/show_bug.cgi?id=6443
#.  "setitimer() should reject noncanonical arguments"
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:275
msgid ""
"POSIX.1-2001 says that B<setitimer>()  should fail if a I<tv_usec> value is "
"specified that is outside of the range [0, 999999].  However, up to and "
"including Linux 2.6.21, Linux does not give an error, but instead silently "
"adjusts the corresponding seconds value for the timer.  From Linux 2.6.22 "
"onward, this nonconformance has been repaired: an improper I<tv_usec> value "
"results in an B<EINVAL> error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/getitimer.2:281
msgid ""
"B<gettimeofday>(2), B<sigaction>(2), B<signal>(2), B<timer_create>(2), "
"B<timerfd_create>(2), B<time>(7)"
msgstr ""

